      "id": 1,
      "text": "Zillmere takes its name from Zillman's Waterholes, named after Lutheran missionary Johann Leopold Zillmann.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.hellotravel.com/australia/zillmere",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "When the North Coast Railway Line reached Petrie in 1888, the new station was christened Zillmere.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zillmere%2C_Queensland",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Eight years after the station opened, Hutton's bacon factory began operations and soon became Zillmere's largest employer.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.queenslandplaces.com.au/zillmere",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hutton's factory operated as a self‑contained complex, complete with on‑site engineering staff and its own electricity generation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.queenslandplaces.com.au/zillmere",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Zillmere Public Library opened its doors in 1971, serving the community for over five decades.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zillmere%2C_Queensland",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Heritage listings in Zillmere include the former St John's Lutheran Church and the Hutton's Factory Workers Cottage.",
